Task 3 – WordPress + MySQL Stack
.env File
MYSQL_ROOT_PASSWORD=supersecret
MYSQL_DATABASE=wordpressdb
MYSQL_USER=wpuser
MYSQL_PASSWORD=wppassword

WORDPRESS_DB_HOST=db:3306
WORDPRESS_DB_USER=wpuser
WORDPRESS_DB_PASSWORD=wppassword
WORDPRESS_DB_NAME=wordpressdb
docker-compose.yml
services:

  db:
    image: mysql:8
    restart: always

    environment:
      MYSQL_ROOT_PASSWORD: ${MYSQL_ROOT_PASSWORD}
      MYSQL_DATABASE: ${MYSQL_DATABASE}
      MYSQL_USER: ${MYSQL_USER}
      MYSQL_PASSWORD: ${MYSQL_PASSWORD}

    volumes:
      - mysql-data:/var/lib/mysql

  wordpress:
    image: wordpress:latest
    restart: always

    ports:
      - "8082:80"

    environment:
      WORDPRESS_DB_HOST: ${WORDPRESS_DB_HOST}
      WORDPRESS_DB_USER: ${WORDPRESS_DB_USER}
      WORDPRESS_DB_PASSWORD: ${WORDPRESS_DB_PASSWORD}
      WORDPRESS_DB_NAME: ${WORDPRESS_DB_NAME}

    depends_on:
      - db

volumes:
  mysql-data:
Architecture
                 docker compose up -d
                           │
                           ▼
                wordpress-lab_default
                           │
          ┌────────────────┴────────────────┐
          │                                 │
          ▼                                 ▼
     wordpress container               mysql container
          │                                 │
          │ WORDPRESS_DB_HOST=db:3306       │
          └────────────connect─────────────►│
                                            │
                                    mysql-data volume
                                            │
                                    /var/lib/mysql
                                            │
                                            ▼
                               Users / Posts / Settings
How Containers Communicate

Compose automatically creates a network:

wordpress-lab_default

Both containers join this network.

Service names become DNS names:

db:

becomes:

db

WordPress connects using:

db:3306

No IP addresses are required.

What Does depends_on Do?
depends_on:
  - db

Only controls startup order.

Start MySQL first
↓
Start WordPress second

Connectivity comes from the Docker network, not depends_on.

Named Volume
volumes:
  - mysql-data:/var/lib/mysql

Stores MySQL data outside the container.

Database survives:

docker compose down
docker compose up -d
Verify Volume
docker volume ls

Example:

wordpress-lab_mysql-data

Inspect:

docker volume inspect wordpress-lab_mysql-data
Persistence Test

After installing WordPress:

Remove containers:

docker compose down

Start again:

docker compose up -d

Result:

WordPress site still exists
User accounts remain
Database remains

Because MySQL files are stored inside:

wordpress-lab_mysql-data
Compose Commands
Start
docker compose up -d
View Containers
docker compose ps
View Logs
docker compose logs
Follow Logs
docker compose logs -f
Logs for Specific Service

WordPress:

docker compose logs wordpress

MySQL:

docker compose logs db
Stop Containers
docker compose stop
Start Stopped Containers
docker compose start
Remove Containers and Network
docker compose down
Remove Containers, Network, and Volumes
docker compose down -v

⚠️ Permanently removes database data.

Key Concepts Learned
Component	Purpose
docker-compose.yml	Defines multi-container applications
Service Names	Internal DNS names
depends_on	Startup order
Networks	Container communication
Named Volumes	Persistent storage
.env File	Externalized configuration
docker compose up	Start stack
docker compose down	Remove stack
docker compose logs	Troubleshooting
docker compose stop/start	Lifecycle management
